  {
    "component": "subtitle",
    "element": {
      "attributes": {
        "class": "cmc-header-3 mb-4",
      },
      "tag": "h3",
    },
    "errors": [],
    "html": "<h3 class='cmc-header-3 mb-4'>The Imperial State Crown</h3>",
    "id": undefined,
    "properties": undefined,
    "text": "The Imperial State Crown",
    "warnings": [],
  },
  {
    "component": "body",
    "element": {
      "attributes": {
        "class": "break-words xs:mb-4",
      },
      "tag": "p",
    },
    "errors": [],
    "html": "<p class='break-words xs:mb-4'>Now she wore the Imperial State Crown, a crown she had also worn at the end of the ceremony. It featured her favourite jewel, the Black Prince’s Ruby, worn by <a href='https://www.discoverbritain.com/history/icons/king-henry-v/' target='_blank' class='text-primary underline' aria-label='Henry V '>Henry V </a>at the Battle of Agincourt. Both this crown, which the Queen later wore on State Openings of Parliament, and the St Edward’s Crown, were heavy, and despite her grace in the carriage, she endured a feat of strength. As she pointed out in the BBC documentary, “You can’t lean down to read your speech [wearing the crown]... Because if you did, your neck would break, and it would fall off.” To protect her posture, she practised wearing it beforehand. Lady Anne Glenconner, another Maid of Honour, recalled: “She used to wear it when she was writing letters. I think Prince Charles says he remembers going in and seeing her wearing it.”</p>",
    "id": undefined,
    "properties": undefined,
    "text": "<p class="break-words xs:mb-4">Now she wore the Imperial State Crown, a crown she had also worn at the end of the ceremony. It featured her favourite jewel, the Black Prince’s Ruby, worn by <a href="https://www.discoverbritain.com/history/icons/king-henry-v/" target="_blank" class="text-primary underline">Henry V </a>at the Battle of Agincourt. Both this crown, which the Queen later wore on State Openings of Parliament, and the St Edward’s Crown, were heavy, and despite her grace in the carriage, she endured a feat of strength. As she pointed out in the BBC documentary, “You can’t lean down to read your speech [wearing the crown]... Because if you did, your neck would break, and it would fall off.” To protect her posture, she practised wearing it beforehand. Lady Anne Glenconner, another Maid of Honour, recalled: “She used to wear it when she was writing letters. I think Prince Charles says he remembers going in and seeing her wearing it.”</p>",
    "warnings": [],
  },
  {
