      },
      "tag": "p",
    },
    "errors": [],
    "html": "<p class='break-words xs:mb-4'>Observers cheered men from the Royal Navy, the Royal Air Force and the Army in the parade, as well as waving to figureheads from across the Commonwealth. But it was Queen Salote of Tonga who captured their hearts, refusing to put the roof up on her carriage, despite the British rain.</p>",
    "id": undefined,
    "properties": undefined,
    "text": "<p class="break-words xs:mb-4">Observers cheered men from the Royal Navy, the Royal Air Force and the Army in the parade, as well as waving to figureheads from across the Commonwealth. But it was Queen Salote of Tonga who captured their hearts, refusing to put the roof up on her carriage, despite the British rain.</p>",
    "warnings": [],
  },
  {
    "component": "subtitle",
    "element": {
      "attributes": {
        "class": "cmc-header-3 mb-4",
      },
      "tag": "h3",
    },
    "errors": [],
    "html": "<h3 class='cmc-header-3 mb-4'>The evening celebrations</h3>",
    "id": undefined,
    "properties": undefined,
    "text": "The evening celebrations",
    "warnings": [],
  },
  {
    "component": "body",
    "element": {
      "attributes": {
        "class": "break-words xs:mb-4",
      },
      "tag": "p",
    },
    "errors": [],
    "html": "<p class='break-words xs:mb-4'>That evening, for the finale, the new Queen stood on the balcony of Buckingham Palace and turned on a show of lights along the Mall. She wore a silver evening gown, an ermine stole and a diamond diadem, and smiled at the thousands of people looking back at her. Beside her stood her husband, the Duke of Edinburgh, in a dinner jacket. At 10.30 pm, fireworks lit up the sky. “Portraits etched in lines of fire depicted the Queen, the Duke of Edinburgh and their two children, Prince Charles, Duke of Cornwall, and Princess Anne,” recorded <i>The New York Times</i>. “Each display brought a surge of cheering and applause.”</p>",
    "id": undefined,
    "properties": undefined,
    "text": "<p class="break-words xs:mb-4">That evening, for the finale, the new Queen stood on the balcony of Buckingham Palace and turned on a show of lights along the Mall. She wore a silver evening gown, an ermine stole and a diamond diadem, and smiled at the thousands of people looking back at her. Beside her stood her husband, the Duke of Edinburgh, in a dinner jacket. At 10.30 pm, fireworks lit up the sky. “Portraits etched in lines of fire depicted the Queen, the Duke of Edinburgh and their two children, Prince Charles, Duke of Cornwall, and Princess Anne,” recorded <i>The New York Times</i>. “Each display brought a surge of cheering and applause.”</p>",
    "warnings": [],
  },
